Rings are understood to be commutative, unless stated otherwise.

#problem()[
  Let $M$ be a module over a commutative ring $R$. Define the _support_ of $M$ (as an $R$-module) to be the set of points $fp in Spec(R)$ for which the localization $M_fp$ is not zero.
  #part()[
    For an ideal $I$ in $R$, show that $supp_R (R\/I) = V(I)$.
  ]
  #part()[
    Show that for a short exact sequence $0 -> A -> B -> C -> 0$ of $R$-modules, the support of $B$ is the union of the supports of $A$ and $C$.
  ]
  #part()[
    Deduce that for any finitely generated $R$-module $M$, the support of $M$ is $V(Ann_R (M))$, the zero set of the annihilator ideal of $M$. (In particular, this is a closed subset of $Spec(R)$.)
  ]
  #part()[
    Give an example to show that if an $R$-module $M$ is not finitely generated, its support need not be closed in $Spec(R)$. (Hint: try $R = ZZ$.)
  ]
]
#solution[Since localization at $fp$ is exact, we know that $(lcoset(R, I))_(fp) iso lcoset(R_(fp), I_(fp))$
  1. Suppose $fp in.not V(I)$. Thus $I subset.eq R - fp$ and $I = I_fp subset.eq R_(fp)^(times)$ under the canonical map $i -> i/1$. Furthermore, this implies that $I_(fp) = R_fp => (lcoset(R, I))_(fp) = 0$. Thus $fp in.not supp_R (lcoset(R, I)) => (fp in supp_R(lcoset(R, I)) => fp in V(I))$. Now we need the reverse implication. Suppose $fp in V(I)$. Note that $R_(fp)$ is a local ring and $fp R_fp$ is the maximal ideal. $ I_fp := {(i) / (s) : i in I, s in R - fp} subset.eq fp R_(fp) subset.neq R_(fp) => (R_(fp)/I_(fp)) != 0 => fp in supp_R ( lcoset(R, I)) $
  2. This is straightforward from the exactness properties of localization.
    - $supp_R (A) union supp_R (C) subset.eq supp_R (B):$ Suppose $fp in supp_R (A)$, then $0 != A_(fp)$ and since $A_(fp) subset.eq B_(fp)$ as a submodule we know that $B_(fp) != 0 => fp in supp_(R)(B_(fp))$. Suppose $fp in supp_R (C) => C_(fp) != 0$, but $C_(fp) iso lcoset(B_(fp), A_(fp)) => B_(fp) != 0 => fp in supp_(R) (B)$
    - Suppose $fp in supp_(R)(B)$ and suppose $fp in.not supp_(R)(A) => A_fp = 0$ and $B_fp != 0$. But since $C_(fp) iso lcoset(B_(fp), A_(fp)) = B_(fp) != 0 => fp in supp_R (C).$ Thus $fp in supp_R (B) => fp in supp_R (A) union supp_R (C)$
    Hence $supp_R (B) = supp_R (A) union supp_R (C)$
  3. We will induct on the minimal number of generators. If $M$ is generated by at least 1 thing, we will call the generator $x$. Then $M iso lcoset(M, Ann_R (x))$ where $Ann_R (x) = {r in R : r x = 0}$. Thus $supp_R (M) = V(Ann_R (x)) = V(Ann_R M)$. Suppose $M$ is generated at least $k$ elements implies the statement is true. Suppose $M$ is generated by at least $k + 1$ elements. Label the elements, $x_1, ..., x_(k + 1)$. Then $lcoset(M, x_(k + 1) M)$ is generated by at most k elements. We have the following short exact sequence $ 0 -> M x_(k + 1) -> M -> lcoset(M, x_(k + 1) M) -> 0 $ which gives us that $ supp_R (M) & = supp_R (M x_(k + 1)) union supp_R (lcoset(M, x_(k + 1) M)) \
               & = supp_R (lcoset(R, Ann_R (x_(k +1 ) ))) union V(Ann_X ( lcoset(M, x_(k + 1) M) )) \
               & = V(Ann_R (x_(k + 1) )) union V(Ann_X ( lcoset(M, x_(k + 1) M) )) \
               & = V( Ann_R (x_(k + 1) ) inter Ann_X ( lcoset(M, x_(k + 1) M) ) ) \
               & = V( Ann_R (M)) $
]

#problem()[
  Let $k$ be a field, and let $R$ be the polynomial ring $k[x, y]$. Give a basis for the $R$-module $M = R\/(x^2, x y)$ as a $k$-vector space. Find a finite filtration of the $R$-module $M$ such that the quotient $R$-modules are isomorphic to $R\/fp_i$ with $fp_i$ prime ideals. What is the support of $M$?
]
#solution[
  Note that $(x^2, x y) = k chevron.l x^n, x^a y^b : n >= 2, a >= 1, b >= 1 chevron.r$ as a $k$ vector space. Thus $M$ has a basis ${1, x, y, y^2, y^3, ... }.$ Let $M_0 = 0$ and $M_1 = k chevron.l arrow(x) chevron.r$ ($x dot arrow(x) = 0$, $y dot arrow(x) = 0$) and $M_2 = M$. We know that $ lcoset(M_1, M_0) & = k chevron.l arrow(x) chevron.r iso lcoset(R, (x, y)) \
  lcoset(M_2, M_1) & = k[y] iso lcoset(R, (x)) $ Note that $(x)$ and $(x, y)$ are prime ideals in $R$. By problem 1, $ supp_R (M_1) & = supp_R (k chevron.l arrow(x) chevron.r) \
               & = V((x, y)) \
               & = {x = 0, y = 0} $

  Thus $ supp_R (M) & = supp_R (M_1) union supp_R (lcoset(R, (x))) \
             & = V((x, y)) union V((x)) \
             & = V((x) (x, y)) \
             & = V((x)) \
             & = {x = 0} $
]

#problem()[
  Show that every factorial domain (also called a unique factorization domain) is normal. So, for example, the integers $ZZ$ and polynomial rings over a field are normal.
]
#solution[
  Let $R$ be a UFD and let $K$ be its fraction field. Let $r in K$ where $ r = p/q $ and $exists F = x^n + sum_(k = 1)^(n - 1) a_k x^k in R[x]$ where $F(r) = 0$. Thus $ (p/q)^n & = -(sum_(k = 1)^(n - 1) a_k p^n q^(- n) ) => p^n = -q^n (sum_(k = 1)^(n - 1) a_k p^n q^(n - k) ) => q^n | p^n => q | p => r in R $

]

#problem()[
  Compute the ring of integers of the number field $QQ(sqrt(5))$. (By definition, this means the integral closure of $ZZ$ in $QQ(sqrt(5))$.)
]
#solution[
  Let $K = QQ(sqrt(5))$. At first glance $cO_K = ZZ[sqrt(5)]$, but note that $x^2 - x - 1 in ZZ[sqrt(5)][x]$ but its root is the golden ratio $1/2 (1 + sqrt(5)) in QQ(sqrt(5))$, thus $ZZ[sqrt(5)]$ isn't integrally closed in $QQ(sqrt(5))$. The answer is $cO_K = ZZ[1/2 (1 + sqrt(5))]$
]
